/reference/k-proof/reference-semantics/semantics/float.k-3-// Minimal, TRUSTED float support. K/Haskell (kprove) has no usable symbolic IEEE-754 theory and
/reference/k-proof/reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:4:// no Int2Float hook, so a float value stays an OPAQUE proof-domain primitive: `intFloatDiv(I, F)`
/reference/k-proof/reference-semantics/semantics/float.k-5-// (the Python true division I / F) is `[no-evaluators]` for kprove — it never reduces on symbolic
/reference/k-proof/reference-semantics/semantics/float.k-6-// input, and the proof verifies only the STRUCTURE (the result is the exact term the program
/reference/k-proof/reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:7:// builds). The `[concrete]` rule evaluates it for krun (llvm HAS the Int2Float / /Float hooks), so
/reference/k-proof/reference-semantics/semantics/float.k-8-// smoke tests run the real float value.
/reference/k-proof/reference-semantics/semantics/float.k-9-//
/reference/k-proof/reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:10:// Only the pieces needed so far: Float as a Val, the float literal, and Int / float true division
/reference/k-proof/reference-semantics/semantics/float.k-11-// (the `a * h / 2.0` idiom). More float ops (float +/-/*, comparison) are added as float problems
/reference/k-proof/reference-semantics/semantics/float.k-12-// need them — kept minimal + sound.
